// Chapter XII: Alice's Evidence — Complete (Pages 176–192)
// Starts on Verso (p. 176). Includes Tipping the Jury-Box (#40 p. 177),
// White Rabbit's 6-stanza Evidence Poem (pp. 183–184), Stepped L-Wrap King & Tart Table (#41 p. 186),
// Flying Pack of Cards (#42 p. 188), and the Closing Dream Sequence + "THE END." (pp. 190–192).

\use "components/Align.dt"
\use "components/VStackStretch.dt"
\use "components/Spacer.dt"
\use "components/Italic.dt"
\use "AliceStyle.dt"

\Span RomanParen(env font: Font) {
    \env font = \Font(font: self.font, italic: false);
    \Children
}

\Component BookEndFooter(
    x: Distance = parent.left,
    y: Distance = parent.top,
    width: Distance = parent.width,
    pagination: Pagination = \Pagination(breakable: false),
    env font: Font
) {
    \Text(
        x: x,
        y: y + 28pt,
        width: width,
        font: \Font(font: font, size: 9.5pt, line_height: 12pt, letter_spacing: 0.6pt),
        color: #111111,
        align: Align.Center,
        text: "THE END."
    )

    \alias right: Distance = x + width;
    \alias height: Distance = 54pt;
    \alias bottom: Distance = y + height;
}

\Component ChapterTwelve(
    x: Distance = parent.left,
    y: Distance = parent.top,
    width: Distance = parent.width,
    pagination: Pagination = \Pagination(breakable: true),
    env font: Font,
    env chapter_meta: PageValue
) {
    \let ch = \AliceChapterPlain(
        x: x,
        y: y,
        width: width,
        number: "XII",
        title: "ALICE’S EVIDENCE",
        verso_header: "ALICE’S EVIDENCE.",
        recto_header: "ALICE’S EVIDENCE.",
        full_header: "ALICE’S EVIDENCE.",
        header_lines: 10.0,
        break_before: Break.Page
    ) {
        // ============================================================
        // Page 176 (Verso): Chapter XII Opener (12 lines)
        // ============================================================
        \Para {
            “H\SmallCaps{ERE}!” cried Alice, quite forgetting in the flurry of the
            moment how large she had grown in the last few minutes, and she
            jumped up in such a hurry that she tipped over the jury\=box with the
            edge of her skirt, upsetting all the jury\-men on to the heads of the
            crowd below, and there they lay sprawling about, reminding her very
            much of a globe of gold\=fish she had accidentally upset the week
            before.
        }

        \Para(align: Align.JustifyAll) {
            “Oh, I \Italic{beg} your pardon!” she exclaimed in a tone of great
            dismay, and began picking them up again as quickly as she could, for
            the acci-
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 177 (Recto, Signature A A): Top Plate #40 (Tipping Jury-Box) + 4 lines
        // ============================================================
        \TopPlate(src: "images/tenniel_40.png", grid_lines: 18.0, img_top: 7pt)

        \FlushPara {
            dent of the gold\=fish kept running in her head, and she had a vague
            sort of idea that they must be collected at once and put back into
            the jury\=box, or they would die.
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Pages 178–182: Rule Forty-Two & the Unopened Verses
        // ============================================================
        \Para(pagination: \Pagination(breakable: true, break_before: Break.Page)) {
            “The trial cannot proceed,” said the King in a very grave voice,
            “until all the jurymen are back in their proper places—\Italic{all,}”
            he repeated with great emphasis, looking hard at Alice as he said
            so.
        }

        \Para {
            Alice looked at the jury\=box, and saw that, in her haste, she had
            put the Lizard in head downwards, and the poor little thing was
            waving its tail about in a melancholy way, being quite unable to
            move. She soon got it out again, and put it right; “not that it
            signifies much,” she said to herself; “I should think it would be
            \Italic{quite} as much use in the trial one way up as the other.”
        }

        \Para {
            As soon as the jury had a little recovered from the shock of being
            upset, and their slates and pencils had been found and handed back
            to them, they set to work very diligently to write out a history of
            the accident, all except the Lizard, who seemed too much overcome to
            do anything but sit with its mouth open, gaz\-ing up into the roof of
            the court.
        }

        \Para {
            “What do you know about this business?” the King said to Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            “Nothing,” said Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            “Nothing \Italic{whatever?}” persisted the King.
        }

        \Para {
            “Nothing whatever,” said Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            “That’s very important,” the King said, turn\-ing to the jury. They
            were just beginning to write this down on their slates, when the
            White Rabbit interrupted: “\Italic{Un}important, your Majesty means,
            of course,” he said in a very respectful tone, but frowning and
            making faces at him as he spoke.
        }

        \Para {
            “\Italic{Un}important, of course, I meant,” the King hastily said,
            and went on to himself in an under\=tone,
            “important—unimportant—unimportant—important\DoubleEmDash{}” as if he were trying
            which word sounded best.
        }

        \Para {
            Some of the jury wrote it down “important,” and some “unimportant.”
            Alice could see this, as she was near enough to look over their
            slates; “but it doesn’t matter a bit,” she thought to herself.
        }

        \Para {
            At this moment the King, who had been for some time busily writing
            in his note\=book, called out “Silence!” and read out from his book,
            “Rule Forty\=two. \Italic{All persons more than a mile high to leave
            the court.}”
        }

        \Para {
            Everybody looked at Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            “\Italic{I’m} not a mile high,” said Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            “You are,” said the King.
        }

        \Para {
            “Nearly two miles high,” added the Queen.
        }

        \Para {
            “Well, I shan’t go, at any rate,” said Alice; “besides, that’s not a
            regular rule: you invented it just now.”
        }

        \Para {
            “It’s the oldest rule in the book,” said the King.
        }

        \Para {
            “Then it ought to be Number One,” said Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            The King turned pale, and shut his note-book hastily. “Consider your
            verdict,” he said to the jury, in a low trembling voice.
        }

        \Para {
            “There’s more evidence to come yet, please your Majesty,” said the
            White Rabbit, jumping up in a great hurry; “this paper has just been
            picked up.”
        }

        \Para {
            “What’s in it?” said the Queen.
        }

        \Para {
            “I haven’t opened it yet,” said the White Rabbit, “but it seems to
            be a letter, written by the prisoner to—to somebody.”
        }

        \Para {
            “It must have been that,” said the King, “unless it was written to
            nobody, which isn’t usual, you know.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Who is it directed to?” said one of the jurymen.
        }

        \Para {
            “It isn’t directed at all,” said the White Rabbit; “in fact, there’s
            nothing written on the \Italic{outside.}” He unfolded the paper as he spoke,
            and added, “It isn’t a letter after all: it’s a set of
            verses.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Are they in the prisoner’s handwriting?” asked another of the
            jurymen.
        }

        \Para {
            “No, they’re not,” said the White Rabbit, “and that’s the queerest
            thing about it.” (The jury all looked puzzled.)
        }

        \Para {
            “He must have imitated somebody else’s hand,” said the King. (The
            jury all brightened up again.)
        }

        \Para {
            “Please your Majesty,” said the Knave, “I didn’t write it, and they
            can’t prove I did: there’s no name signed at the end.”
        }

        \Para {
            “If you didn’t sign it,” said the King, “that only makes the matter
            worse. You \Italic{must} have meant some mischief, or else you’d
            have signed your name like an honest man.”
        }

        \Para {
            There was a general clapping of hands at this: it was the first
            really clever thing the King had said that day.
        }

        \Para {
            “That \Italic{proves} his guilt,” said the Queen.
        }

        \Para {
            “It proves nothing of the sort!” said Alice. “Why, you don’t even
            know what they’re about!”
        }

        \Para {
            “Read them,” said the King.
        }

        \Para {
            The White Rabbit put on his spectacles. “Where shall I begin, please
            your Majesty?” he asked.
        }

        \Para {
            “Begin at the beginning,” the King said, gravely, “and go on till you
            come to the end: then stop.”
        }

        \Para {
            These were the verses the White Rabbit read:—
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 183 (Recto): Stanzas 1–4 of the Evidence Poem
        // ============================================================
        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 15.5pt, italic: true, break_before: Break.Page) {
            \Spacer(height: 2pt)
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { “They told me you had been to her, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { And mentioned me to him: }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { She gave me a good character, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { But said I could not swim. }

            \Spacer(height: 36pt)

            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { He sent them word I had not gone }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { \RomanParen{(}We know it to be true\RomanParen{)}: }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { If she should push the matter on, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { What would become of you? }

            \Spacer(height: 36pt)

            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { I gave her one, they gave him two, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { You gave us three or more; }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { They all returned from him to you, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { Though they were mine before. }

            \Spacer(height: 36pt)

            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { If I or she should chance to be }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { Involved in this affair, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { He trusts to you to set them free, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { Exactly as we were. }
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Pages 184–185: Stanzas 5–6 & Dissecting the Verses
        // ============================================================
        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 15.5pt, italic: true, break_before: Break.Page) {
            \Spacer(height: 2pt)
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { My notion was that you had been }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { (Before she had this fit) }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { An obstacle that came between }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { Him, and ourselves, and it. }

            \Spacer(height: 36pt)

            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { Don’t let him know she liked them best, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { For this must ever be }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 0pt) { A secret, kept from all the rest, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 44pt, indent: 14.5pt) { Between yourself and me.” }
            \Spacer(height: 32pt)
        }

        \Para {
            “That’s the most important piece of evidence we’ve heard yet,” said
            the King, rubbing his hands; “so now let the jury\DoubleEmDash{}”
        }

        \Para {
            “If any one of them can explain it,” said Alice, (she had grown so
            large in the last few minutes that she wasn’t a bit afraid of
            interrupt\-ing him,) “I’ll give him sixpence. \Italic{I} don’t believe
            there’s an atom of meaning in it.”
        }

        \Para {
            The jury all wrote down on their slates, “\Italic{She} doesn’t
            believe there’s an atom of meaning in it,” but none of them
            attempted to explain the paper.
        }

        \Para {
            “If there’s no meaning in it,” said the King, “that saves a world of
            trouble, you know, as we needn’t try to find any. And yet I don’t
            know,” he went on, spreading out the verses on his knee, and looking
            at them with one eye; “I seem to see some meaning in them, after
            all. ‘\Italic{—said I could not swim—}’ you can’t swim, can you?” he
            added, turning to the Knave.
        }

        \Para {
            The Knave shook his head sadly. “Do I look like it?” he said. (Which
            he certainly did \Italic{not,} being made entirely of cardboard.)
        }

        \Para {
            “All right, so far,” said the King, and he went on mut\-ter\-ing over
            the verses to him\-self: “‘\Italic{We know it to be true—}’ that’s the
            jury, of course—‘\Italic{I gave her one, they gave him two—}’ why,
            that must be what he did with the tarts, you know—”
        }

        \Para {
            “But it goes on ‘\Italic{they all returned from him to you,}’” said
            Alice.
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 186 (Verso): Stepped L-Wrap #41 (King & Tart Table)
        // Narrow King on top-left (13 lines on right) + full-width Tart Table (lines 14–19) + 3 lines below
        // ============================================================
        \WrapLeftStack(
            src: "images/tenniel_41.png",
            img_offset_x: -9.5pt,
            img_offset_y: 9pt,
            narrow_x: 129pt,
            min_grid_lines: 19.0,
            break_before: Break.Page
        ) {
            \Para {
                “Why, there they are!” said the King tri\-um\-phantly, point\-ing to
                the tarts on the table. “Noth\-ing can be clear\-er than
                \Italic{that.} Then again—‘\Italic{before she had this fit—}’
                you never had fits, my dear, I think?” he said to the Queen.
            }
            \Para(align: Align.JustifyAll) {
                “Never!” said the Queen fu\-ri\-ously, throw-
            }
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Pages 186 (bottom 3 lines) -> 187: "Sentence first—verdict afterwards."
        // ============================================================
        \FlushPara {
            ing an inkstand at the Lizard as she spoke. (The unfortunate little
            Bill had left off writing on his slate with one finger, as he found
            it made no mark; but he now hastily began again, using the ink, that
            was trickling down his face, as long as it lasted.)
        }

        \Para {
            “Then the words don’t \Italic{fit} you,” said the King, looking
            round the court with a smile. There was a dead silence.
        }

        \Para {
            “It’s a pun!” the King added in an angry tone, and everybody
            laughed. “Let the jury consider their verdict,” the King said, for
            about the twentieth time that day.
        }

        \Para {
            “No, no!” said the Queen. “Sentence first—verdict afterwards.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Stuff and nonsense!” said Alice loudly. “The idea of having the
            sentence first!”
        }

        \Para {
            “Hold your tongue!” said the Queen, turn\-ing purple.
        }

        \Para {
            “I won’t!” said Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            “Off with her head!” the Queen shouted at the top of her voice.
            Nobody moved.
        }

        \Para {
            “Who cares for you?” said Alice, (she had grown to her full size by
            this time.) “You’re nothing but a pack of cards!”
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 188 (Verso): Top Plate #42 (Flying Pack of Cards) + 2 lines
        // ============================================================
        \TopPlate(src: "images/tenniel_42.png", grid_lines: 20.0, img_top: 7pt)

        \Para {
            At this the whole pack rose up into the air, and came flying down upon
            her; she gave a little scream, half of fright and half of
            anger, and tried to beat them off, and found herself lying on the
            bank, with her head in the lap of her sister, who was gently
            brushing away some dead leaves that had fluttered down from the trees
            on to her face.
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 189 (Recto): Waking up on the river bank
        // ============================================================
        \Para {
            “Wake up, Alice dear!” said her sister; “why, what a long sleep
            you’ve had!”
        }

        \Para {
            “Oh, I’ve had such a curious dream!” said Alice, and she told her
            sister, as well as she could remember them, all these strange
            Ad\-ventures of hers that you have just been read\-ing about; and when
            she had finished, her sis\-ter kissed her, and said, “It \Italic{was}
            a curious dream, dear, certainly: but now run in to your tea; it’s
            getting late.” So Alice got up and ran off, thinking while she ran,
            as well she might, what a wonderful dream it had been.
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Pages 190–192: Closing Dream Sequence (Double Rule + Bare Centered Folios)
        // ============================================================
        \VStackStretch(
            width: parent.width,
            gap: 0pt,
            pagination: \Pagination(breakable: false, break_before: Break.Page, keep_with_next: true)
        ) {
            \Spacer(height: 46pt)
            \DoubleRule(rule_width: 66pt, gap: 2.5pt)
            \Spacer(height: 50pt)
        }

        \Para {
            But her sister sat still just as she left her, leaning her head on
            her hand, watching the setting sun, and thinking of little Alice and
            all her wonderful Adventures, till she too began dreaming after a
            fashion, and this was her dream:—
        }

        \Para {
            First, she dreamed of little Alice herself:—once again the tiny
            hands were clasped upon her knee, and the bright eager eyes were
            look\-ing up into hers—she could hear the very tones of her voice, and
            see that queer little toss of her head to keep back the wandering
            hair that \Italic{would} always get into her eyes—and still as she
            listened, or seemed to listen, the whole place around her became
            alive with the strange creatures of her little sister’s dream.
        }

        \Para {
            The long grass rustled at her feet as the White Rabbit hurried
            by—the frightened Mouse splashed his way through the neighbouring
            pool—she could hear the rattle of the teacups as the March Hare and
            his friends shared their never\=ending meal, and the shrill voice of
            the Queen ordering off her unfortunate guests to execution—once more
            the pig\=baby was sneezing on the Duchess’ knee, while plates and
            dishes crashed around it—once more the shriek of the Gryphon, the
            squeaking of the Lizard’s slate-pencil, and the choking of the
            suppressed guinea-pigs, filled the air, mixed up with the distant
            sob of the miserable Mock Turtle.
        }

        \Para {
            So she sat on, with closed eyes, and half believed herself in
            Wonderland, though she knew she had but to open them again and all
            would change to dull reality—the grass would be only rustling in the
            wind, and the pool rip\-pling to the waving of the reeds—the rattling
            teacups would change to tinkling sheep\=bells, and the Queen’s shrill
            cries to the voice of the shepherd boy—and the sneeze of the baby,
            the shriek of the Gryphon, and all the other queer noises, would
            change (she knew) to the con\-fused clamour of the busy farm\=yard—while
            the lowing of the cattle in the distance would take the place of the
            Mock Turtle’s heavy sobs.
        }

        \Para {
            Lastly, she pictured to herself how this same little sister of hers
            would, in the after\=time, be herself a grown woman; and how she
            would keep, through all her riper years, the simple and loving heart
            of her childhood: and how she would gather about her other little
            children, and make \Italic{their} eyes bright and eager with many a
            strange tale, perhaps even with the dream of Wonderland of long\=ago:
            and how she would feel with all their simple sorrows, and find a
            pleasure in all their simple joys, remembering her own child\=life,
            and the happy summer days.
        }

        \BookEndFooter()
    };

    \alias right: Distance = x + width;
    \alias height: Distance = ch.height;
    \alias bottom: Distance = y + height;
}
